                                                         Question 1 — How serious is the churn problem?
What percentage of customers have churned, stayed, or recently joined, and what customer revenue or monthly charges are associated with the churned population?


                               Query:
SELECT
    COUNT(*) total_customers,
    SUM(CASE
        WHEN Customer_Status ='Churned' THEN 1
        ELSE 0 END) AS chunred_customers,

    ROUND(
        SUM(CASE WHEN Customer_Status ='Churned'
        THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)* 100.0/COUNT(*),2) AS churn_rate,

    SUM(CASE
    WHEN Customer_Status ='Churned' THEN Monthly_Charge
    ELSE 0 END) AS monthly_churned,

    SUM(CASE WHEN Customer_Status ='Churned' THEN Total_Revenue
    ELSE 0 END) AS total_revenue
FROM telecom_customer;
Findings;
26.54% of customers have churned, and they were associated with 3,684,459.8200 in historical total revenue, meaning roughly 1 in 4 customers have left the company.
This represents an important retention problem that could affect recurring revenue of the company.

                                Possible Drivers:
Further analysis shows that our customers churned mostly because of competitor-related issues 

SELECT
    Churn_Category,
    COUNT(*) AS churned_customers,
    ROUND(
        COUNT(*) * 100.0 /
        SUM(COUNT(*)) OVER (),
        2
    ) AS percentage_of_churn
FROM telecom_customer
WHERE Customer_Status = 'Churned'
GROUP BY Churn_Category
ORDER BY churned_customers DESC;

                                        Recommendation:
Trying to improve onboarding process for our customers and make the benefit of our services and product more visible to our customers can be  a way to reduce competitors related 
churn issues or any other issues regarding our services and product


